EXT. INK RIVER - FERRYMAN'S TOLL BOOTH - TWILIGHT

Black water. Slow. Letters swim in it like minnows. A crooked booth on stilts.

MAGDA ORLOVSKAYA-FELL, 60s, tollkeeper, lantern for a left hand, counts coins into a jar.

BASIL THORNQUIST, 30s, pilgrim, muddy cloak, stands at the booth window. A satchel at his feet.

SIX-FINGERED JO, ageless, a heron the height of a man, stalks along the dock.

MAGDA
Toll's a memory.

BASIL
A memory?

MAGDA
Of your own. Not borrowed.

Basil digs in his satchel. Pulls out a pebble. Holds it up.

BASIL
My mother's garden.

Magda sniffs the pebble. Shakes her head.

MAGDA
Too small.

BASIL
It's a good memory.

MAGDA
It's a pebble.

Jo ruffles. Taps the dock with a long beak.

JO
Let him pass, Magda.

MAGDA
Don't tell me my business, bird.

JO
He's carrying something heavy.

MAGDA
They all are.

Basil lifts the satchel. Sets it on the sill. The booth creaks.

BASIL
What if I told you what's inside?

Magda and Jo lean in.

MAGDA
Tell.

JO ^
Tell.

They share a look. Both clear their throats. Basil smiles faintly.

BASIL
A name.

MAGDA
Whose?

JO ^
Whose?

BASIL
Mine. I left it at home.

Magda writes in her ledger. Crosses out a line.

MAGDA
That'll do.

She slides a coin through. It's blank on both sides. Basil takes it.

JO
Mind the third current.

BASIL
Thank you.

The ferry is a hollow boat of reeds. It nudges the dock. Basil steps aboard.
